{"answerArea":{"calculator":true,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"Let's try to find the limit using direct substitution.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x^2+1}\u0026=\\dfrac{(-1)^2-9}{(-1)^2+1}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{-8}{2}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-4\n\\end{align}$\n\nWe got a finite number.\n\nSince $\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x^2+1}$ is continuous across its domain, we can determine that $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x^2+1}$ is indeed equal to $-4$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion, $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x^2+1}=-4$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Find $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x^2+1}$.**\n\n[[☃ radio 1]]\n\n","images":{},"widgets":{"radio 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"choices":[{"content":"$-4$","correct":true},{"content":"$-5$","correct":false},{"content":"$-9$","correct":false,"isNoneOfTheAbove":false},{"content":"The limit doesn't exist","correct":false,"isNoneOfTheAbove":false}],"deselectEnabled":false,"displayCount":null,"hasNoneOfTheAbove":false,"multipleSelect":false,"randomize":false},"static":false,"type":"radio","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
